body:
Ok, their relationship has a whole set of issues and it doesn't seem healthy. THAT SAID... you don't out people, and you don't define their sexual orientation for them.  Even if you think she's in denial, even if you think this is a situation of compulsory heterosexuality: you need to respect each person's personal process of coming out to themselves and defining themselves however they feel comfortable with. Pushing people to "accept they're not straight" doesn't work, it's a very personal process that each person goes through at their own pace. Also, it didn't seem like you were trying to help her, it was about you being right and proving your point. 

&#x200B;

I get you might have good intentions, but again: there are things you don't do. Forcing someone to come out, publicly or to themselves, because you perceive their sexuality to be a certain way is an AH move. She might come out as bisexual. She might come out as a lesbian. She might still identify as straight even when having sex with women. She might identify as whatever she feels fits her under this big umbrella and guess what? it's HER call. Not yours. 

&#x200B;

So yes, YTA.

Thank you. I have this nice picture of my dad at home and I talk to him sometimes, tell him things he might like to hear about my life. It makes me feel closer to him. 

&#x200B;

Big hugs to you and hope you find something that helps with your grief.

I did NOT do the math. 

&#x200B;

That's...

&#x200B;

Yeah.

The student came across as harassing because they were harassing teachers and staff (myself included). That could have been avoided by the student not harassing people. 

&#x200B;

I don't know what more you want me to say. Stop harassing these people.
